{
  "id": "s01-l01",
  "unit": "s01-vowels",
  "title": "Vowels 1: অ আ ই ঈ",
  "tip": "**Bengali has 11 independent vowel letters (স্বরবর্ণ shorborno).** They are used when a vowel *starts* a syllable, e.g. at the start of a word.\n\n- **অ** = `o`, a short open 'o' as in *or*. Every consonant secretly carries this sound, so you will see অ everywhere later.\n- **আ** = `a` as in *father*. Mnemonic: আ is অ plus an extra stick (া), and that stick is exactly the sign that adds 'a' to a consonant later.\n- **ই** and **ঈ** are both `i` as in *see*. Their names are *hroshsho-i* ('short i') and *dirgho-i* ('long i'), but **in everyday speech they sound the same**. You just learn which one each word is spelled with.\n\nTip: all four letters 'hang' from a top line (মাত্রা matra), which is how Bengali letters join into words.",
  "vocab": [
    {
      "id": "l-o",
      "bn": "অ",
      "tr": "o",
      "en": "o as in 'or' (short, open)",
      "notes": "Name: sworo-o (স্বরে অ). Also the 'inherent vowel' hidden in every consonant."
    },
    {
      "id": "l-a",
      "bn": "আ",
      "tr": "a",
      "en": "a as in 'father'",
      "notes": "Name: sworo-a (স্বরে আ). Looks like অ with an extra stroke (া) on the right."
    },
    {
      "id": "l-i",
      "bn": "ই",
      "tr": "i",
      "en": "i as in 'see' (short-i letter)",
      "notes": "Name: hroshsho-i (হ্রস্ব ই) = 'short i'. Sounds the same as ঈ in modern speech."
    },
    {
      "id": "l-ii",
      "bn": "ঈ",
      "tr": "i",
      "en": "i as in 'see' (long-i letter)",
      "notes": "Name: dirgho-i (দীর্ঘ ই) = 'long i'. Same sound as ই; only the spelling differs."
    },
